$$\n\nSo, less than half of David's goals scored were from the penalty spot.\n\n- Statement “Lionel had a greater percentage of goals scored from the penalty spot compared to David” is true. We can compare $12%$ and $0.1$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.1\\times 100=10%\n$$\n\nSince  $12%>10%,$ Lionel had a **greater** percentage of goals scored from the penalty spot compared to David.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Less than half of Lionel's goals scored were from the penalty spot”; “Lionel had a greater percentage of goals scored from the penalty spot compared to David” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186148115 1071 "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186148115 1071 "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n- **I.** $f(x)=2x+1$\n- **II.** $g(x,y)={x}^{3}+3xy$\n- **III.** $h(x,y)={x}^{5}-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{5}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186148124 1071 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$\nII. $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2$\nIII. $2x+7$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186148124 1071 "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x,y)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186148168 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=10{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t+ϕ)+{e}^{-2t}$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186148168 1071 "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+9y=10{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186148168 1074 "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nOnly $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$ and $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$ match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$; $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186148168 1074 "The general solution to this equation is the sum of the complementary function and a particular solution.\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$\nIt can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=9\\,⟹\\,\\omega =3.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =1.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+{e}^{-t}$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{e}^{-t}$\n\nOnly I and III match the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186148175 1071 "Which of the fol