 recorded the height and shoe size of the players on the basketball team he coaches. The information he collected is summarized in the scatter plot above. \n\nGiven that none of the points overlap, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152419 1074 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement I is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose vertical coordinate equals $9.5,$ namely, $(180,9.5)$ and $(195,9.5).$ This means that two players have a shoe size of  $9\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2058bf02025ff359b509db01022c5d2e956d67243094a33692b90d6f1c2eac8.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement II is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $195,$ namely, $(195,\\,9.5).$ This means that only one of the players has a height of $195\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8b4364dc5de11eed12840bc644b1392b36160adfdf739b96f282c2367d999fa.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement III is false. According to the scatter diagram, three players have a height greater than  $185\\,\\text{cm},$ represented by the three dots to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/aa/aaf6d496c56c3cdc5a47e1c09e7640d7a3caa05446a75b705c042a56c116cb98.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152419 1074 "Let's examine our scatter plot.\n\n- Statement “Only one player has a shoe size of $9\\frac{1}{2}.$” is false. The scatter plot has two dots whose vertical coordinate equals $9.5,$ namely, $(180,9.5)$ and $(195,9.5).$ This means that two players have a shoe size of  $9\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n![](images/c2/c2058bf02025ff359b509db01022c5d2e956d67243094a33692b90d6f1c2eac8.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement “Only one player is $195\\,\\text{cm}$ tall” is true. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $195,$ namely, $(195,\\,9.5).$ This means that only one of the players has a height of $195\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8b4364dc5de11eed12840bc644b1392b36160adfdf739b96f282c2367d999fa.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement “There are exactly two players taller than $185\\,\\text{cm}.$” is false. According to the scatter diagram, three players have a height greater than  $185\\,\\text{cm},$ represented by the three dots to the right of the vertical line.\n\n![](images/aa/aaf6d496c56c3cdc5a47e1c09e7640d7a3caa05446a75b705c042a56c116cb98.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Only one player is $195\\,\\text{cm}$ tall” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152435 1071 "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x-1<x.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152435 1071 "$\\,$ $P(x):$ $x-1<x.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$\n- **II.** The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$\n- **III.** $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152435 1074 "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & <x \\\\ -1 & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the final condition is true for all $x\\in Z,$ we have that the truth set of our predicate is ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152435 1074 "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & <x \\\\ -1 & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the final condition is true for all $x\\in Z,$ we have that the truth set of our predicate is ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “The truth set of $P(x)$ is $∅.$” is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement “The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$” is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=Z.$\n\n- Statement $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$ is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The truth set of $P(x)$ is $Z.$”; $∀x\\in Z,\\,P(x).$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152449 1071 "Let $m\\in N$ and $n\\in N.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152449 1071 "Let $m\\in N$ and $n\\in N.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n.$\n- **II.** The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$\n- **III.** $∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152449 1074 "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option $∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all natural $m$ and $n,$ we have $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n.$ is true, since $1\\le n$ for all natural numbers $n.$\n\n- Statement “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$” is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(m)$ is true for some $m\\in N$ and false for others. For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true. However, \n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,2\\le n\n$$\n\nis false, since $2≰1.$\n\n- Statement $∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n.$ is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀m\\in N\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∀n\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $N.$ But, according to statement “The truth set of the predicate $(\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)$ is $N.$”, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n.$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152449 1074 "First, notice that the statement \n\n$$\n∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nfrom option III reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all natural $m$ and $n,$ we have $m\\le n.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, since $1\\le n$ for all natural numbers $n.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For the predicate\n\n$$\nP(m):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\n$$\n\nwe note that $P(m)$ is true for some $m\\in N$ and false for others. For example, \n\n$$\nP(1):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\n$$\n\nis true. However, \n\n$$\nP(2):\\,∀\\,n\\in N,\\,2\\le n\n$$\n\nis false, since $2≰1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The sentence \n\n$$\n(\\,∀\\,m,n\\in N,\\,m\\le n\\,)\n$$\n\n \ncan be written as\n\n$$\n∀m\\in N\\,(\\,\\underbrace{∀n\\in N,\\,m\\le n}_{P(m)}\\,)\n$$\n\nwhich is true whenever the inner predicate $P(m)$ is true over the universal set $N.$ But, according to statement II, this is not the case.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152453 1071 "$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152453 1071 "$\\,$ ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0$\n\nWhich of the following conditions are necessary for the above to be true?\n- **I.** $x=1$\n- **II.** $x<0$\n- **III.** $|x|<5$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152453 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nNote that the solution to the given equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition $x=1$ is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇏\\,(x=1)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=-3,$ then ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0,$ but $x\\ne 1.$\n\n- Condition $x<0$ is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇏\\,(x<0)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $x=1,$ then ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0,$ but $x≮0.$\n\n- Condition $|x|<5$ is a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-3=0)\\,⇒\\,(|x|<5)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nEach solution of ${x}^{2}+2x-3=0$ satisfies $|x|<5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $|x|<5$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152453 1074 "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, 