hich of the following equivalence classes contain the element $i?$\n\nI. $[-1]$\n\nII. $[-i]$\n\nIII. $[2i]$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152754 1071 "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $i?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152754 1074 "We have $i\\in [x]$ if $i∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{4} & ={x}^{4} \\\\ 1 & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $i\\in [-1]$ because $(-1)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\in [-i]$ because $(-i)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\notin [2i]$ because $(2i)^{4}=16\\ne 1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152754 1074 "We have $i\\in [x]$ if $i∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{4} & ={x}^{4} \\\\ 1 & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $i\\in [-1]$ because $(-1)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\in [-i]$ because $(-i)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\notin [2i]$ because $(2i)^{4}=16\\ne 1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $[-1]$; $[-i]$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152758 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152758 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n- **II.** $22≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$\n- **III.** $20≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152758 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ is false because $4∤(19-5).$ Notice that $19-5=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement $22≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$ is true because $3∣(22-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(22-7)÷3=15÷3=5\n$$\n\n- Statement $20≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$ is false because $8∤(20-9).$ Notice that $20-5=15,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $22≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152758 1074 "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $4∤(19-5).$ Notice that $19-5=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $3∣(22-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(22-7)÷3=15÷3=5\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $8∤(20-9).$ Notice that $20-5=15,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152767 1071 "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152767 1071 "Which of the following functions are surjections?\n- **I.** $f:{0,1,2}\\to {1,3,9,27}$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$\n- **II.** $g:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$\n- **III.** $h:{0,1,2}\\to {-1,1,3}$ defined by $h(x)=2x-1$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152767 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $27$ from the codomain. The only number $x$ that satisfies $f(x)={3}^{x}=27$ is $x=3,$ but $3\\notin {0,1,2}.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $g(x)=-1.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=-1$ is $x=-1,$ but $-1\\notin [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- III is a surjection. Indeed, $h(0)=-1,$ $h(1)=1$ and $h(2)=3.$ So, for every $y\\in {-1,1,3},$ there exists an element $x\\in {0,1,2}$ such that $h(x)=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152767 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- “$f:{0,1,2}\\to {1,3,9,27}$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $27$ from the codomain. The only number $x$ that satisfies $f(x)={3}^{x}=27$ is $x=3,$ but $3\\notin {0,1,2}.$\n\n- “$g:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{3}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $g(x)=-1.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=-1$ is $x=-1,$ but $-1\\notin [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- “$h:{0,1,2}\\to {-1,1,3}$ defined by $h(x)=2x-1$” is a surjection. Indeed, $h(0)=-1,$ $h(1)=1$ and $h(2)=3.$ So, for every $y\\in {-1,1,3},$ there exists an element $x\\in {0,1,2}$ such that $h(x)=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$h:{0,1,2}\\to {-1,1,3}$ defined by $h(x)=2x-1$” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152792 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[6]_{9}?$\n\nI. $-12$\n\nII. $3$\n\nIII. $24$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152792 1071 "Which of the following integers belong to the residue class $[6]_{9}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152792 1074 "The residue class $[6]_{9}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $6$ modulo $9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[6]_{9} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=6+9k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{6+9k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,6-2\\cdot 9,\\,6-9,\\,6,\\,6+9,\\,6+2\\cdot 9,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-12,-3,6,15,24,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-12$ and $24$ belong to $[6]_{9},$ while $3$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152792 1074 "The residue class $[6]_{9}$ consists of the elements that are congruent to $6$ modulo $9.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[6]_{9} & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x≡6\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\\} \\\\  & =\\{x\\in Z\\,|\\,x=6+9k,\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{6+9k\\,|\\,k\\in Z\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,\\,6-2\\cdot 9,\\,6-9,\\,6,\\,6+9,\\,6+2\\cdot 9,\\,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{…,-12,-3,6,15,24,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-12$ and $24$ belong to $[6]_{9},$ while $3$ does not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number $-12$”; “The number $24$” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152809 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152809 1071 "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n- **I.** $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$\n- **II.** $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- **III.** $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152809 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a function and the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152809 1074 "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{2}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does 