/51/5130380c907723f29eb6232b5de8ea1fb93716c20df983a47725cb3e29e71702.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152842 1071 "![](images/51/5130380c907723f29eb6232b5de8ea1fb93716c20df983a47725cb3e29e71702.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n- **I.** $f$ is a surjection\n- **II.** $f$ is an injection\n- **III.** $f$ is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152842 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, according to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$ So, $f$ is a surjection.\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since both statements I and II are true, $f$ is a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152842 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is a surjection” is true. Indeed, according to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$ So, $f$ is a surjection.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is an injection” is true. As we can see from the diagram, there are no two distinct elements within $X$ that map to the same element in $Y.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ As a result, $f$ is an injection.\n\n- Statement “$f$ is a bijection” is true. Indeed, since both statements “$f$ is a surjection” and “$f$ is an injection” are true, $f$ is a bijection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f$ is a surjection”; “$f$ is an injection”; “$f$ is a bijection”." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152878 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186152878 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-2$ is a natural number\n\n- **II.** $-2$ is an integer\n\n- **III.** $-2$ is a rational number" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152878 1074 "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-2$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-2$ is an integer.\n\n- $-2$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-2)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186152878 1074 "Let's recall our definitions.\n\n- The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers: $1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…$\n\n- The integers consist of all of the positive and negative whole numbers, and zero: $…,-3,\\,-2,\\,-1,\\,0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$\n\n- The rationals consist of any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $-2$ is not a natural number.\n\n- $-2$ is an integer.\n\n- $-2$ is a rational number because it can be written as $\\frac{(-2)}{1}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is, “$-2$ is an integer”; “$-2$ is a rational number” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186157753 1071 "Suppose $f(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186157753 1071 "Suppose $f(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- **II.** $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- **III.** $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186157753 1074 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+2{e}^{2x})^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1+4{e}^{2x}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+4{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{2x})^{′}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{3x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2{e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{3x})^{′}}+0 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186157753 1074 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+2{e}^{2x})^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1+4{e}^{2x}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+4{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{2x})^{′}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement “$f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{3x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2{e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{3x})^{′}}+0 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $”; “$f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186157794 1071 "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186157794 1071 "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n- **I.** $¬A∧B$\n- **II.** $¬(A∨¬B)$\n- **III.** $A∨¬B$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186157794 1074 "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A∧B\\,$ | $\\,¬(A∨¬B)\\,$ | $\\,A∨¬B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬A∧B$ and $¬(A∨¬B)$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186157794 1074 "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\,¬A∧B\\,$ | $\\,¬(A∨¬B)\\,$ | $\\,A∨¬B\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬A∧B$ and $¬(A∨¬B)$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $¬A∧B$; $¬(A∨¬B)$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186157800 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $X?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186157800 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $X?$\n- **I.** $X∨(X∨Y)$\n- **II.** $(Y∧X)∨X$\n- **III.** $Y∨(X∧Y)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186157800 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nX∨(X∧Y)≡X.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement $X∨(X∨Y)$ is *not* logically equivalent to $X.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement $(Y∧X)∨X$ is logically equivalent to $X.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Y∧X)∨X & ≡X∨(Y∧X) \\\\  & ≡X∨(X∧Y) \\\\  & ≡X.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement $Y∨(X∧Y)$ is *not* logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∨(X∧Y) & ≡Y∨(Y∧X) \\\\  & ≡Y≢X.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $(Y∧X)∨X$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186157800 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nX∨(X∧Y)≡X.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $X.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $X.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Y∧X)∨X & ≡X∨(Y∧X) \\\\  & ≡X∨(X∧Y) \\\\  & ≡X.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $A.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y∨(X∧Y) & ≡Y∨(Y∧X) \\\\  & ≡Y≢X.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186