$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=0,\n$$\n\n we obtain that $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(1,2).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)}>0,\n$$\n\n we have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186157980 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,2),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(2{x}^{3}-y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =2(1)^{3}-(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =6(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3}+(2) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=0,\n$$\n\n we obtain that $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement “$(1,2)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$” is false. There is a critical point at $(1,2).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)}>0,\n$$\n\n we have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement “$(1,2)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$” is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186157990 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,e)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186157990 1071 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,e)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $(0,e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- **II.** $(0,e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- **III.** $(0,e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186157990 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,e),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {e}^{x}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(y{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}+e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =2e \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)}=e\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,e)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is no critical point at $(0,e),$ therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,e).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186157990 1074 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,e),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {e}^{x}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(y{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\  & =y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,e)} & ={(y{e}^{2x}+y{e}^{x})|}_{(0,e)} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{2(0)}+e\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =2e \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$(0,e)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$” is false. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,e)}=e\\ne 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,e)$ is not a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement “$(0,e)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$” is false. There is no critical point at $(0,e),$ therefore we cannot have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement “$(0,e)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$” is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,e).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162235 1071 "![](images/b3/b3c5df109b00450c20acdbc07378bfe309cf808c1244bedd6ff4fa3a850ccd13.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nI. $f(10)=25$\n\nII. $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n\nIII. The function $f:X\\to Y$ is not **into**\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162235 1071 "![](images/b3/b3c5df109b00450c20acdbc07378bfe309cf808c1244bedd6ff4fa3a850ccd13.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162235 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $f(10)=25$ is true.  Indeed,   there is an arrow from $10\\in X$ to $25\\in Y.$ So,  $f(10)=25.$\n\n- Statement “$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$” is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement “The function $f:X\\to Y$ is not **into**” is true. Since statement “$∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$” is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(10)=25$; “The function $f:X\\to Y$ is not **into**” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162235 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed,   there is an arrow from $10\\in X$ to $25\\in Y.$ So,  $f(10)=25.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162251 1071 "Which of the following matrices are Jordan blocks?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162251 1071 "Which of the following matrices are Jordan blocks?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162280 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nConsider the function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Which of the following conditions are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162280 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nConsider the function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above. Which of the following conditions are true?\n- **I.** $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$\n- **II.** ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$\n- **III.** $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162280 1074 "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition I is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition II is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.1+0.25+0.25+0.05+0.15+0.2=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is true. Because $f(x,y)$ satisfies conditions I and II, it is a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162280 1074 "Let's check the given conditions:\n\n- Condition “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” is true. Every value of $f(x,y)$ in the table satisfies $0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1.$\n\n- Condition ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$ is true. Adding up all the values of $f(x,y)$ shown in the table, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y) & =0.1+0.25+0.25+0.05+0.15+0.2=1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function” is true. Because $f(x,y)$ satisfies conditions “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$” and ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$, it is a valid probability mass function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$0\\le f(x,y)\\le 1$ for all $(x,y)$ in $S$”; ${\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}f(x,y)=1$; “$f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability mass function”." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162464 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162464 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $6∣(42x+24y)$\n- **II.** $7∣(42x+24y)$\n- **III.** $2∣(42x+24y)$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162464 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $6∣(42x+24y)$ is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=6(7x+4y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $7x+4y$ must be an integer, too. So, $6∣(42x+24y).$\n\n- Statement $7∣(42x+24y)$ is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $42(0)+24(1)=24,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement $2∣(42x+24y)$ is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=2(21x+12y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $21x+12y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2∣(42x+24y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $6∣(42x+24y)$; $2∣(42x+24y)$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162464 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=6(7x+4y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $7x+4y$ must be an integer, too. So, $6∣(42x+24y).$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $42(0)+24(1)=24,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=2(21x+12y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $21x+12y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2∣(42x+24y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162567 1071 "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nI. $f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$\n\nII. $g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{4}$\n\nIII. $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={5}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162567 1071 "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162567 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{2}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{2}⁡y)={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- II is an into function. Let's take $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only numbers $x$ that satisfy $g(x)={x}^{4}=2$ are $x=\\pm \\sqrt[4]{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={5}^{x}=-1.$ In fact, ${5}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162567 1074 "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- “$f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={2}^{x}$” is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{2}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{2}⁡y)={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- “$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{4}$” is an into function. Let's take $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only numbers $x$ that satisfy $g(x)={x}^{4}=2$ are $x=\\pm \\sqrt[4]{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- “$h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={5}^{x}$” is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={5}^{x}=-1.$ In fact, ${5}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{4}$”; “$h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={5}^{x}$” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162568 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={3}^{x}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162568 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={3}^{x}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$\n\n- **II.** $∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\n- **III.** $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162568 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/a2/a2a6352a60b420f8721d6c7e4553d3157cfb045a39b6a5119c11c5c8189023e2.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values greater than $y=0$, excluding $y=0$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Take, for example, $y=-1.$ Since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ),$ there can' t be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the function is not a surjection.  So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162568 1074 "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/a2/a2a6352a60b420f8721d6c7e4553d3157cfb045a39b6a5119c11c5c8189023e2.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$” is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values greater than $y=0$, excluding $y=0$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement “$∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$” is false. Take, for example, $y=-1.$ Since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ),$ there can' t be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $-1.$\n\n- Statement “$f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$” is true. Since statement “$∀\\,y\\in R,$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$” is false, the function is not a surjection.  So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$”; “$f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162592 1071 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 7 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162592 1071 "Which of the following could be matrices of quadratic forms defined on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162613 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162613 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162634 1071 "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162634 1071 "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n\n- **II.** $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}x$\n\n- **III.** $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162634 1074 "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}:$\n\n- Quadratic form $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ *cannot* be positive-definite since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-10 \\\\  & =-5<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}x$ *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -20\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =45>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-2 \\\\  & =3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162634 1074 "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}∖{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *cannot* be positive-definite since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-10 \\\\  & =-5<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -20\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =45>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-2 \\\\  & =3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162637 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162637 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is indefinite" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162637 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-7,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162637 1074 "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=7$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$” is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(-6-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-7,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” is false, while statement “$A$ is indefinite” is true. Since $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is indefinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is indefinite” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162638 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162638 1071 "Consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=9$ and $det(A)=18$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is negative-definite" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162638 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=9$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=9>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162638 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=9$ and $det(A)=18$” is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=9$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18.\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” is true, while statement “$A$ is negative-definite” is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=9>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}|=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are positive-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${a}_{11}=9$ and $det(A)=18$”; “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is positive-definite” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162639 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162639 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $A$ is positive-definite\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162639 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162639 1074 "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)>0$” is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1$ but \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite” is true, while statement “$A$ is positive-definite” is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162676 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162676 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- **II.** The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- **III.** The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162676 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162676 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric”; “The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162694 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186162694 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- **II.** The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- **III.** If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162694 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186162694 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric” is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric”; “If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167051 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167051 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=x{e}^{xy}\\,i+y{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(xy+{y}^{2})\\,i+(xy+{x}^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,j$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167051 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=y{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=xy+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=xy+{x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)$ and $Q=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167051 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x{e}^{xy}$ and $Q=y{e}^{xy}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{xy},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=xy+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=xy+{x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x+2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)$ and $Q=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $H=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,j$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167085 1071 "![](images/1b/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$\nII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\nIII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167085 1071 "![](images/1b/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167087 1071 "![](images/bb/bba667daa6bfd40eeb5450cc86066184819b903ace2641bc58c6b5c83869a4b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167087 1071 "![](images/bb/bba667daa6bfd40eeb5450cc86066184819b903ace2641bc58c6b5c83869a4b6.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4y+4$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167087 1074 "![](images/72/72801ec62e5e23173097ef674169b26c85fab65cbf543af44b3582f0e14fd0c4.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y=y(y-2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4y+4=(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=2$ only.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}=y(2-y)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167087 1074 "![](images/72/72801ec62e5e23173097ef674169b26c85fab65cbf543af44b3582f0e14fd0c4.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y=y(y-2)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4y+4=(y-2)^{2}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=2$ only.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-{y}^{2}=y(2-y)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167088 1071 "![](images/66/660feebe44e179b53be85d23c497e58de4774258b588a6f8ac785a0fd9c8fc08.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2y-{y}^{2}-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}+y}{y-1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{y-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167088 1071 "![](images/66/660feebe44e179b53be85d23c497e58de4774258b588a6f8ac785a0fd9c8fc08.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167157 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167157 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n- **II.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n- **III.** ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167157 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$ and ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$ are decoupled since their corresponding matrices are diagonal.\n\n- System ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$ is *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$; ${x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167157 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nIn matrix form, a linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ is decoupled if $A$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I and III are decoupled since their corresponding matrices are diagonal.\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because its matrix is not diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167158 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167158 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n- **I.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n- **II.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n- **III.** $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167158 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Systems $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=\\pi {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167158 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- Systems I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- Systems III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167178 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167178 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n- **I.** $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- **III.** $-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167178 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $2$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option $-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$; $-4\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167178 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x