 homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the first term is not a constant.\n\n- Option II is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167265 1071 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167265 1071 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **II.** $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **III.** $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167265 1074 "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $f$ is $\\pi $-periodic, shifting by $2\\pi $ does not change the value of $f,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f((x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\pi )=f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does not always equal $f(x).$ For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=F(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\ne f(x)$ for $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167265 1074 "Since $f$ is the $\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(x+\\pi ))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement “$f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” is false. Since $f$ is $\\pi $-periodic, shifting by $2\\pi $ does not change the value of $f,$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})=f((x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\pi )=f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does not always equal $f(x).$ For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=F(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{2})\\ne f(x)$ for $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$”; “$f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167319 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167319 1071 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=({x}^{2}+2y)\\,i+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(y{e}^{x}+2)\\,i+({e}^{x}+4y)\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=y\\,i+x\\,j$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167319 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q={x}^{2}+2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y{e}^{x}+2$ and $Q={e}^{x}+4y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y$ and $Q=x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167319 1074 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P={x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q={x}^{2}+2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y{e}^{x}+2$ and $Q={e}^{x}+4y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=y$ and $Q=x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $G=(y{e}^{x}+2)\\,i+({e}^{x}+4y)\\,j$; $H=y\\,i+x\\,j$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167389 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{2}=2y+{y}^{3}.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167389 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{2}=2y+{y}^{3}.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n- **I.** $y=0$\n- **II.** $y=1$\n- **III.** $y=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167389 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}+2(0)=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)={1}^{3}+{1}^{2}+2(1)=4\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)={2}^{3}+{2}^{2}+2(2)=16\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167389 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}+2(0)=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)={1}^{3}+{1}^{2}+2(1)=4\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)={2}^{3}+{2}^{2}+2(2)=16\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y=0$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167399 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-7y+6.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167399 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-7y+6.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${y}_{1}(x)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- **II.** The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and ${y}_{3}(x)$\n- **III.** ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167399 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}-7y+6.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}-7(1)+6=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We know that $y=1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y-1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-1)({y}^{2}+y-6) \\\\  & =(y-1)(y-2)(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=2,\\,{y}_{3}(x)=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=1+2+(-3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167399 1074 "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}-7y+6.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement “${y}_{1}(x)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation” is true.  We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}-7(1)+6=0.$\n\n- Statement “The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and ${y}_{3}(x)$” is true. We know that $y=1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y-1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-1)({y}^{2}+y-6) \\\\  & =(y-1)(y-2)(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=2,\\,{y}_{3}(x)=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=0$ is true. We have ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=1+2+(-3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${y}_{1}(x)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation”; “The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and ${y}_{3}(x)$”; ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=0$." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167440 1071 "Which of the following are third-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167440 1071 "Which of the following are third-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{‴}+3x{y}^{′}=xy$\n- **II.** $3{y}^{″}{y}^{′}-2xy=2{y}^{′}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-2x={y}^{″}$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167440 1074 "A differential equation is of third-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{‴}+3x{y}^{′}=xy$ is a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\n- Equation $3{y}^{″}{y}^{′}-2xy=2{y}^{′}$ is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-2x={y}^{″}$ is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{‴}+3x{y}^{′}=xy$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167440 1074 "A differential equation is of third-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167441 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167441 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- **II.** $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- **III.** $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167441 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$ is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$ is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$ is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$; $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167441 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167442 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167442 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}-2xy={e}^{y}$\n- **III.** $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167442 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System $2x{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1$ is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2x{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System ${y}^{′}-2xy={e}^{y}$ is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$ is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $2x{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167442 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2x{y}^{′}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167454 1071 "![](images/76/76c15fa584f5a216d23ef10b10fbae14c09b84714e49bd8db0cc7954cab8c0f3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167454 1071 "![](images/76/76c15fa584f5a216d23ef10b10fbae14c09b84714e49bd8db0cc7954cab8c0f3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167454 1074 "![](images/11/111e679939475cabbfe9a40779de3fa1bf4f2de732f943a3b0396a3ee9bf3519.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2$ only.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ gives the required behavior.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167454 1074 "![](images/11/111e679939475cabbfe9a40779de3fa1bf4f2de732f943a3b0396a3ee9bf3519.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=2$ only.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)^{2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ gives the required behavior.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167458 1071 "![](images/68/680bc0001fb95783ee70fc88a7106997bcb3dba2ad85a708164878d0f610ecfb.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{5x}{x-2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167458 1071 "![](images/68/680bc0001fb95783ee70fc88a7106997bcb3dba2ad85a708164878d0f610ecfb.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167458 1074 "![](images/74/74b482b5b72421c9ee208034dc4727605e42874015d080130ffd7d18437af0da.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to 2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{5x}{x-2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x}{x-2}=\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{2}{x})}\\to 5\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x-2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167458 1074 "![](images/74/74b482b5b72421c9ee208034dc4727605e42874015d080130ffd7d18437af0da.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=2.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to 2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{5x}{x-2}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x}{x-2}=\\frac{5}{(1-\\frac{2}{x})}\\to 5\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-2}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x-2}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \