d as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nI. The number of correct answers in a multiple choice test of $10$ questions, where each answer is either \"true\" or \"false,\" and each answer is guessed randomly.\n\nII. The number obtained when spinning a fair roulette wheel.\n\nIII. The initial letter of a word picked at random from an English dictionary.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167516 1071 "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186167516 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each number of correct answers is not equally likely. For example, it is more likely to score $50%$ than $100%.$\n\n- Random variable II can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. For example, each outcome of a roulette wheel is represented by a number between $0$ and $37,$ and since the roulette is fair, each number is equally likely.\n\n- Random variable III cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because some letters are more common than others.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186167516 1074 "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of correct answers in a multiple choice test of $10$ questions, where each answer is either \"true\" or \"false,\" and each answer is guessed randomly” cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each number of correct answers is not equally likely. For example, it is more likely to score $50%$ than $100%.$\n\n- Random variable “The number obtained when spinning a fair roulette wheel” can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. For example, each outcome of a roulette wheel is represented by a number between $0$ and $37,$ and since the roulette is fair, each number is equally likely.\n\n- Random variable “The initial letter of a word picked at random from an English dictionary” cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely, because some letters are more common than others.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number obtained when spinning a fair roulette wheel” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172278 1071 "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172278 1071 "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **II.** $H(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- **III.** $K(x,y,z)=x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172278 1074 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172278 1074 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$; $H(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{xy}\\,j$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172279 1071 "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172279 1071 "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $G(t)={e}^{t}\\,i-t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$\n- **II.** $H(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **III.** $K(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172279 1074 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172279 1074 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $K(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172299 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172299 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨y,\\,-x⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,1)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the parabola $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path.\n\n- **IV.** $F$ is conservative." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172299 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement IV is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172299 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨t,\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t-t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the parabola $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨{t}^{2},\\,-t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is independent of path” is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172300 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x+y},\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172300 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x+y},\\,{e}^{x+y}⟩$ and the points $O(0,0)$ and $A(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F$ is conservative.\n\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$\n\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172300 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172300 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x+y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{0} \\\\  & =e\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-1 \\\\  & =2e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$”; “${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1),$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$”." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172569 1071 "![](images/35/35f52e6a2c3eafe59fea3c14351067fdf4b78b8c2f247454429952eb3a6f8270.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=2xy$\n- ${y}^{′}=-xy$\n- ${y}^{′}=x+1$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172569 1071 "![](images/35/35f52e6a2c3eafe59fea3c14351067fdf4b78b8c2f247454429952eb3a6f8270.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172569 1074 "![](images/e3/e3afa2080ea81f679c9c9b3423cfbe7b11bed5625854a0a33ff01312ca3629e4.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the righ