eld become horizontal at $y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=x+y+1$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x+y+1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ but \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}\\to \\frac{y}{2}<\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x-1}$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x-1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ and \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=|\\frac{y}{x-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x-1}$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172570 1074 "![](images/b8/b8a9560870e28c751b8869e298ffabdbcc23eec720c7a1057d55032a62767183.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x+y+1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ but \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}\\to \\frac{y}{2}<\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x-1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ and \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=|\\frac{y}{x-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172602 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $3?$\n\nI. $187\\,349$\n\nII. $3\\,121\\,557$\n\nIII. $99\\,125\\,041$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172602 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $3?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172602 1074 "A number is divisible by $3$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $3.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $187\\,349$ is\n\n$$\n1+8+7+3+4+9=32,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* divisible by $3.$ So, $187\\,349$ is *not* divisible by $3.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $3\\,121\\,557$ is\n\n$$\n3+1+2+1+5+5+7=24,\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $3.$ So, $3\\,121\\,557$ is divisible by $3.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $99\\,125\\,041$ is\n\n$$\n9+9+1+2+5+0+4+1=31,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* divisible by $3.$ So, $99\\,125\\,041$ is *not* divisible by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172602 1074 "A number is divisible by $3$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $3.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $187\\,349$ is\n\n$$\n1+8+7+3+4+9=32,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* divisible by $3.$ So, $187\\,349$ is *not* divisible by $3.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $3\\,121\\,557$ is\n\n$$\n3+1+2+1+5+5+7=24,\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $3.$ So, $3\\,121\\,557$ is divisible by $3.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $99\\,125\\,041$ is\n\n$$\n9+9+1+2+5+0+4+1=31,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* divisible by $3.$ So, $99\\,125\\,041$ is *not* divisible by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $3\\,121\\,557$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172617 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The amount of time until the next earthquake in a particular country\n\nII. The number of giraffes in a zoo\n\nIII. The number of defective bottles produced in a factory in a single day\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172617 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nSelect all that apply." 131