roduced in a factory in a single day” does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of defective bottles produced is discrete, not continuous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The amount of time until the next earthquake in a particular country” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172625 1071 "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172625 1071 "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?\n- **I.** ${x}_{1}^{2}=6$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{5}=-1$\n- **III.** ${x}_{2}^{2}=-7$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172625 1074 "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}=6$ defines a non-empty set. It represents two parallel lines, ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{6}$ and ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{5}=-1$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{2}^{2}=-7$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172625 1074 "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}=6$ defines a non-empty set. It represents two parallel lines, ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{6}$ and ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{5}=-1$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{2}^{2}=-7$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${x}_{1}^{2}=6$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172633 1071 "![](images/dc/dcee145044fe79dc7f4d07c3c4c1248df358cd8eda7a173cd8d4641f13404122.png)\n\nConsider the function $f:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The domain of $f$ is ${0,1,2,3}$\n- The codomain of $f$ is ${0,-1}$\n- The range of $f$ is ${0,-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172633 1071 "![](images/dc/dcee145044fe79dc7f4d07c3c4c1248df358cd8eda7a173cd8d4641f13404122.png)\n\nConsider the function $f:X\\to Y$ given by the mapping diagram shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172633 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X={0,1,2,3},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y={0,-1,-2},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is ${0,-1},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172633 1074 "The notation $f:X\\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nThe range of $f$ is the subset of the codomain that contains all values $y\\in Y$ that are mapped to by at least one element $x\\in X.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect our diagram:\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X={0,1,2,3},$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y={0,-1,-2},$\n\n- the range of $f$ is ${0,-1},$ the subset of elements of $Y$ that have at least one incoming arrow.\n\nAs a result, statements “The domain of $f$ is ${0,1,2,3}$” and “The range of $f$ is ${0,-1}$” are true, while statement “The codomain of $f$ is ${0,-1}$” is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer “The domain of $f$ is ${0,1,2,3}$”; “The range of $f$ is ${0,-1}$” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172639 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (x - 1)(y + 2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172639 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (x - 1)(y + 2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **II.** At every point $(x, y)$ where $x < 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **III.** At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y < - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172639 1074 "For (x-1)(y+2), both factors are positive in region I. In regions II and III one factor is negative and one positive. Only statement I is true." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172639 1074 "For (x-1)(y+2), both factors are positive in region “At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive”. In regions “At every point $(x, y)$ where $x < 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive” and “At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y < - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive” one factor is negative and one positive. Only statement “At every point $(x, y)$ where $x > 1$ and $y > - 2$, the slope of the slope field is positive” is true." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172642 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nI. The time interval between successive incidents at a particular crossroads\n\nII. The number of incidents in a month at a particular crossroads\n\nIII. The speed of a car that arrives at a particular crossroads\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172642 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled by an exponential distribution?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172642 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Incidents can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next incident occurs.\n\n- Random variable II does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of incidents in a month is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172642 1074 "The exponential distribution can be thought of as the continuous analog of the geometric distribution. It models the amount of continuous time or space until a particular event occurs, where the events should occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The time interval between successive incidents at a particular crossroads” could be modeled using an exponential distribution. Incidents can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know the amount of time until the next incident occurs.\n\n- Random variable “The number of incidents in a month at a particular crossroads” does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There is no interval of continuous time or space. The number of incidents in a month is discrete, not continuous.\n\n- Random variable “The speed of a car that arrives at a particular crossroads” does *not* follow an exponential distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The time interval between successive incidents at a particular crossroads” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172645 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186172645 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **II.** At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **III.** At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186172645 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=2xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ef852c9c15dc6f591119906abe479949f929edb6dc26f21588b4de545d02da2.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statemen