$ and $y>2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive” is true. If $x<-1$ and $y>2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<-1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $x<-1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement “At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>-1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative” is true. If $x>-1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176448 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\cos x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176448 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\cos x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The function has maximum values at $x = \\frac{π}{2} + nπ$.\n- **II.** The function is zero at $x = \\frac{3π}{2}$.\n- **III.** The range of the function is $[- π, π]$." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176448 1074 "Cosine attains its maxima at x=2n*pi, vanishes at x=3pi/2, and has range [-1,1]. Only statement II is true." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176448 1074 "Cosine attains its maxima at x=2n*pi, vanishes at x=3pi/2, and has range [-1,1]. Only statement “The function is zero at $x = \\frac{3π}{2}$” is true." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176449 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\cos x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176449 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\cos x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The zeros of the function are $x = \\frac{π}{2} + nπ$.\n- **II.** The function has maximum values at $x = \\frac{3π}{2} + 2nπ$.\n- **III.** The range of $y = \\cos x$ is $[- 1, 0]$." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176449 1074 "Cosine has zeros at x=pi/2+n*pi. At x=3pi/2+2n*pi it is zero, not maximal, and its range is [-1,1]. Only I is true." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176449 1074 "Cosine has zeros at x=pi/2+n*pi. At x=3pi/2+2n*pi it is zero, not maximal, and its range is [-1,1]. Only “The zeros of the function are $x = \\frac{π}{2} + nπ$” is true." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176452 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176452 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$\n- **II.** The function returns only positive values on the intervals $(\\pi n,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n)\\,.$\n- **III.** The function has zeros at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176452 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has an asymptote at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and the period is $\\pi .$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function returns only positive values on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the period is $\\pi .$ Therefore, the function is positive on $(n\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )\\,.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi .$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176452 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement “The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$” is false. The function has an asymptote at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and the period is $\\pi .$ Therefore, the asymptotes occur at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement “The function returns only positive values on the intervals $(\\pi n,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n)\\,.$” is true. The function returns only positive values on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the period is $\\pi .$ Therefore, the function is positive on $(n\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )\\,.$\n\n- Statement “The function has zeros at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$” is false. The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=n\\pi .$\n\nSo, the correct answer is “The function returns only positive values on the intervals $(\\pi n,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n)\\,.$” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176453 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\tan x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176453 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\tan x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The function has asymptotes at $x = \\frac{π}{2} + πn$.\n- **II.** The range of the function is $y ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})$.\n- **III.** The domain of the function is all real numbers except $x = nπ$." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176453 1074 "Tangent has vertical asymptotes at pi/2+n*pi and range all real numbers. Its domain excludes pi/2+n*pi, rather than n*pi. Only I is true." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176453 1074 "Tangent has vertical asymptotes at pi/2+n*pi and range all real numbers. Its domain excludes pi/2+n*pi, rather than n*pi. Only “The function has asymptotes at $x = \\frac{π}{2} + πn$” is true." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176476 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176476 1071 "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ to the origin. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(T)=1$,  where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$\n- **II.** $\\text{Im}(T)$ contains no vectors of ${R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176476 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace ${0},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The image of $T$ contains the vector zero vector $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176476 1074 "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is the zero-subspace ${0},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=0\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$\\text{rank}(T)=1$,  where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$” is false. In our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\text{Im}(T)$ contains no vectors of ${R}^{2}$” is false. The image of $T$ contains the vector zero vector $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}$ is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)={R}^{2}$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176487 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\csc x$? Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176487 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\csc x$? Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The point $(π,-1)$ is not on the graph.\n- **II.** The function has a vertical asymptotes at $x = nπ$.\n- **III.** The value $y = 2$ is in the range of the function." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176493 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-1, & x<0.5 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2}, & x\\ge 0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176493 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-1, & x<0.5 \\\\ 4-{x}^{2}, & x\\ge 0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $(0,1)$\n- **II.** $[0,2]$\n- **III.** $[1,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176493 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(0,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(x-1)=(0.5)-1=-0.5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(4-{x}^{2})=4-(0.5)^{2}=3.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0.5$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.5.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[1,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0.5,$ which is not in the interval $[1,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176493 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $(0,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,2].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(x-1)=(0.5)-1=-0.5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0.5}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0.5}(4-{x}^{2})=4-(0.5)^{2}=3.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0.5$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.5.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[0,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[1,2].$ The only discontinuity of the function $f(x)$ is at $x=0.5,$ which is not in the interval $[1,2].$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[1,2],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[1,2]$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176494 1071 "![](images/2a/2ae902b1cccc00b59401ecba306ef7f6f47e92f6439614c13c349283d9e763ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which interval is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\n- $[-1,2]$\n- $(1,3]$\n- $[-4,-1]$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176494 1071 "![](images/2a/2ae902b1cccc00b59401ecba306ef7f6f47e92f6439614c13c349283d9e763ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. On which interval is the extreme value theorem applicable to $f(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176494 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,2]$ is closed, but the function is not defined at $x=2.$ So, it can't be continuous at this point. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-4,-1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-4,-1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176494 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $[-1,2]$ is closed, but the function is not defined at $x=2.$ So, it can't be continuous at this point. Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $(1,3]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[-4,-1]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[-4,-1].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[-4,-1]$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176496 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x?$ \n\nI. $(0,5]$\n\nII. $[1,5]$\n\nIII. $[0,5]$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176496 1071 "On which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function $y={log}_{2}⁡x?$ \n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176496 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,5]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[0,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176496 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at each interval.\n\n- The interval $(0,5]$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- The interval $[1,5]$ is closed, and the function is continuous on $[1,5].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable.\n\n- The interval $[0,5]$ is closed, but the function is not continuous on $[0,5]$ because this interval contains points where the function is not defined. In particular, it is not defined at $x=0.$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is $[1,5]$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176497 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,{x}^{2}+1, & x<1 \\\\ -x+2, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176497 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,{x}^{2}+1, & x<1 \\\\ -x+2, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n- **I.** $[0,2]$\n- **II.** $(1,2)$\n- **III.** $[-2,2]$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176507 1071 "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176507 1071 "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ converges at  $x=-1.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n- **I.** The series converges at $x=0$\n- **II.** The series converges at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$\n- **III.** The series diverges at $x=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176507 1074 "The center of the expansion is $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{3}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-1,2).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-1,2).$\n\n![](images/03/03901ede0b4fadb59556c63aa4534b3a9e20c1e8188388e6256434ece03d9211.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $0\\in [-1,2),$ the series is convergent at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-\\frac{3}{2}\\notin [-1,2),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $2\\notin [-1,2),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186176507 1074 "The center of the expansion is $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-1,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $\\frac{3}{2},$ i.e., $R\\ge \\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-1,2).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-1,2).$\n\n![](images/03/03901ede0b4fadb59556c63aa4534b3a9e20c1e8188388e6256434ece03d9211.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement “The series converges at $x=0$” is true. Since $0\\in [-1,2),$ the series is convergent at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement “The series converges at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$” is false. Since $-\\frac{3}{2}\\notin [-1,2),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- Statement “The series diverges at $x=2$” is false. Since $2\\notin [-1,2),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The series converges at $x=0$” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176548 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\csc x$? Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186176548 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y = \\csc x$? Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The domain of the function is all real numbers except $x = nπ$.\n- **II.** The function has a zero at $x = \\frac{π}{2}$.\n- **III.** The function takes the value $-1$ at $x =-\\frac{π}{2} + 2nπ$." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186179988 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y=u+v-1.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $det(A)=-2$\n\nIII. The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186179988 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=v-u,\\,y=u+v-1.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186179988 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-u \\\\ u+v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-u+v \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement $det(A)=-2$ is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1=-2.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving” is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $det(A)=-2$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186179988 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-u \\\\ u+v-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-u+v \\\\ u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1=-2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186179993 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186179993 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-v,\\,y=u+2v+1.\n$$\n\nSuppose that $A$ is the matrix that represents the linear transformation associated with $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **II.** $det(A)=0$\n- **III.** The transformation is invertible" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186179993 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement $det(A)=0$ is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The transformation is invertible” is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The transformation is invertible” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186179993 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ u+2v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}u-v \\\\ u+2v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=1\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)\\ne 0,$ the affine transformation is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186180004 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=3u-v+2,\\,y=v-u.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186180004 1071 "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=3u-v+2,\\,y=v-u.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- **II.** $det(A)=-2$\n\n- **III.** The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186180004 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v+2 \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement $det(A)=-2$ is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement “The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving” is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186180004 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v+2 \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=3\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186180019 1071 "![](images/14/145b9dcc1e52dec65d64245afa89fba77e0d0cda5a919b52009e2397f3f03e16.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186180019 1071 "![](images/14/145b9dcc1e52dec65d64245afa89fba77e0d0cda5a919b52009e2397f3f03e16.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186180019 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is false, while statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/20/20121d30445c9e756f1c2c024713bc6cd6f0464cfcfc24df68bc30c766c4988e.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/0f/0ff98536c275458d68deabda91f56bf63e7c8f59f5ea79c68292332d8cd58d4f.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$; ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186180019 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/20/20121d30445c9e756f1c2c024713bc6cd6f0464cfcfc24df68bc30c766c4988e.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/0f/0ff98536c275458d68deabda91f56bf63e7c8f59f5ea79c68292332d8cd58d4f.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186180020 1071 "![](images/50/501a3512fb4b8a1898c6dfb8816d587de4a0cd585991d9c92d2bb35d83a2853d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186180020 1071 "![](images/50/501a3512fb4b8a1898c6dfb8816d587de4a0cd585991d9c92d2bb35d83a2853d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186180020 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is false while statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c11b38f9e598e0cd8fc50715d2091e5bea48d188440ae8a99c84d66a6acb6b9.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/34/346007ae582fccdf4519a3d1cd50a467e19d5345238343a1311fd3348bbeb138.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$; ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true]