{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186180084 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186180084 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n- **I.** $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$\n- **II.** $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **III.** $F(x,y)=2x\\,i-y\\,j$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186180084 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0+0=0\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y+2y\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\,i-y\\,j$ doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+(-1)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186180084 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0+0=0\n$$\n\n- Vector field II doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=y+2y\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2+(-1)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186184603 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186184603 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-2{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186184603 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x-2{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2{e}^{x} & >0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c199b110a3d19ecc23454f52dc7470af318ce871805595fd5fe7609dee5b0545.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that ${e}^{x}<1$ when $x<0.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${e}^{x}>1,$ which has the solution $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186184603 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x-2{e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2{e}^{x} & >0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y={e}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c199b110a3d19ecc23454f52dc7470af318ce871805595fd5fe7609dee5b0545.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that ${e}^{x}<1$ when $x<0.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(-\\infty ,0).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${e}^{x}>1,$ which has the solution $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$”; “$f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186184608 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186184608 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- **II.** $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$\n- **III.** $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186184608 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $(0,\\pi ).$\n\n![](images/41/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0$ when $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ which has the solution $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186184608 1074 "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $(0,\\pi ).$\n\n![](images/41/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0$ when $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ which has the solution $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186184875 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186184875 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** Any $3\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- **II.** The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ has one negative singular value.\n- **III.** Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186184875 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{2\\times 3}\\times {A}_{3\\times 2}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 2}\\times {A}_{2\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186184875 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “Any $3\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values” is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $2\\times 2$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{2\\times 3}\\times {A}_{3\\times 2}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{2\\times 2}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement “The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ has one negative singular value” is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement “Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values” is true. Since $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 2}\\times {A}_{2\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values” only." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186184880 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537062 17592186576411 true] [17592186184880 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** Any $6\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values\n- **II.** Any $10\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values.\n- **III.** The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has two non-negative singular values." 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186184880 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $A$ is a $10\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 10$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 10}\\times {A}_{10\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, all singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139537062 17592186576411 false] [17592186184880 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “Any $6\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values” is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement “Any $10\\times 8$ matrix $A$ has at most $10$ distinct singular values” is false. Since $A$ is a $10\\times 8$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $8\\times 10$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $8\\times 8$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{8\\times 10}\\times {A}_{10\\times 8}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{8\\times 8}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $8$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement “The matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has two non-negative singular values” is true.  Indeed, all singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is